WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.310
I’m at the Arecibo Observatory in Puerto
Rico.

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:06.970
It is one of the world’s largest radio telescopes,
with a dish 300 metres across.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:11.220
And I had seen pictures and video of this
before I came here,

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:15.860
but, honestly, the sheer scale is impossible
to get across on camera.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:16.860
It is massive.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.539
For 50 years, it’s been listening to the
sky, and one day,

00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:22.130
it might just save the world.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:26.140
Arecibo’s been part of discovering the large-scale
structure of the universe.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.710
We’ve discovered pre-biotic molecules in
distant galaxies,

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:33.200
the first millisecond pulsar, the first exoplanets.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.660
The big one, which everyone knows about,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:38.020
is the Nobel Prize for the discovery of the
binary pulsar

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:41.230
and the way that its orbit decayed exactly
as predicted by general relativity.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.110
There's 50 years of discoveries here,

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.050
I could talk for 50 years on
discoveries of Arecibo.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.970
One of the most incredible things about the
observatory is this,

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.610
the suspended platform that I’m standing
on right now.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:56.080
This entire colossal structure is suspended
in the air on cables,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.870
like a suspension bridge,
there are no pillars underneath

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.829
holding this up and getting in the way.

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:05.579
All this weight is being carried by the three
concrete towers around the dish.

00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:09.479
And this platform isn’t static:
big parts of it move.

00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:12.729
The telescope, by going a short way
north and south,

00:01:12.729 --> 00:01:15.450
can actually see about a third of the total
sky from here.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.649
The main bowl of this dish is spherical.

00:01:17.649 --> 00:01:18.979
The complication, though, is:

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:22.649
a spherical mirror doesn’t have a point
of focus, like a hyperbolic mirror,

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:26.579
which we’re used to with big optical telescopes
and most big radio telescopes.

00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:28.900
And so, in order to get the signal to a focus,

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.399
the Gregorian dome contains two smaller reflectors.

00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:34.829
These smaller reflectors actually bring everything
to a point focus.

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:38.719
That means, in the Gregorian dome,
we can have much wider band receivers

00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:42.869
and cover from 0.3GHz to 10GHz in frequency.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:47.469
Arecibo can do something that most
radio telescopes can’t: it can transmit.

00:01:47.469 --> 00:01:51.700
Famously, in 1974, it beamed the Arecibo message
out to the stars,

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.759
but there are also some more practical uses

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:56.990
for sending big pulses of energy into space:
radar.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:02.000
Arecibo can bounce radar signals off planets,
asteroids and, well,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.799
anything nearby in the solar system.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:07.700
And it can build up clear pictures of what's
out there in the dark.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.660
The advantage of using the radar imaging is

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.540
you can actually see the shape much better
compared to optical imaging.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.450
We can actually see boulders on the surface
if they are close enough,

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.130
if it looks like a bone or
if it looks like a... sweet potato!

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.970
The S-band transmitter is a high-powered transmitter

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:30.070
that works at 2380MHz of frequency,
that’s the S-band.

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:32.900
The whole transmitter's a megawatt of power.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.630
This transmitter produces a beam of power,

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.570
and if you put your hand right through it
you could get burned.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:41.570
We mostly observe near-Earth asteroids.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:45.820
When new asteroids are found their orbit is
not very well defined yet,

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:50.070
the main thing that we can do is really enhance
the orbit information.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:54.810
You transmit a signal and
when it hits a moving object

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:58.400
the wavelength of the signal
changes depending on

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.790
if the asteroid is moving towards the observer
or away from the observer.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:08.710
In that way we can actually get an estimate,
to millimetres per second,

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:10.540
of the velocity of the asteroid.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:13.930
When new asteroids are found the distance
can be determined

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.940
to hundreds of metres, even tens of metres.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.100
We can get very accurate information of where
the asteroid will be

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:26.560
from now to a decade forward,
or even a century forward.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.820
Asteroid Apophis, the preliminary orbiting
information showed

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.120
that it actually might hit the Earth.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:38.700
After it was observed with radar they could
determine it will come super-close,

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.690
it will probably disturb some of the satellites
that are orbiting Earth,

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.310
but it doesn’t hit the Earth.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.740
That’s very important to know, because now

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:54.290
we know we don’t have to send
Bruce Willis to deflect the asteroid(!)

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.500
Of all natural catastrophes,

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:00.100
impacts are the one thing
that we can actually avoid.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:05.430
Earthquakes, volcanoes, they just happen and
we can’t actually help that.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:09.260
But if we can observe an asteroid early enough

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:14.840
we have an opportunity to deflect it.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.970
Thank you so much to everyone
at the Arecibo Observatory.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:20.040
If you’d like to know more about them
and their discoveries

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.639
pull down the description for lots of links,

00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:23.910
and if you want to see behind the scenes
on this video

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:27.160
then Matt, who’s on camera, has been taking
a load of footage

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.010
and has put it together for the second channel.

